<DocsLayout
  title="Pipeline autonomy — Kvendra developer docs"
  description="The declarative pipeline-autonomy policy of the Skills orchestrators: default two human gates, single-gate, zero-gate with an autonomy log and an inviolable hard floor, and backlog chaining."
>
    <span class="kvd-kicker">Concepts</span>
    <h1>Pipeline autonomy</h1>
    <p>
      The pipeline orchestrators of the Skills plugin — <code>/new-feature</code>
      and <code>/bug</code> — pause for a human at fixed points. How many pauses
      a project wants is a decision, so it is declared once in the knowledge
      base as a policy, not repeated in every conversation. The policy is
      <strong>opt-in</strong>: with no policy, every pipeline keeps the most
      conservative behaviour.
    </p>
    <p class="kvd-callout">
      The policy governs <strong>conversation gates and orchestration
      only</strong>. It never relaxes broker enforcement, the deploy no-go list,
      the plugin hooks or the transaction protocol. Someone able to edit it
      could remove a conversational checkpoint, never run a credentialed
      operation.
    </p>

    <h2>Where the policy lives</h2>
    <p>
      An active <code>STD</code> entity tagged <code>scope:pipeline-autonomy</code>
      at project level, optionally with a component-level override. At start the
      orchestrator runs exactly one query for it, reads it fresh on every run,
      and prints the resolved mode in its progress header:
    </p>
    <pre><code>autonomy: zero-gate | single-gate | dual-gate (policy: &lt;STD-id&gt; v&lt;N&gt; | defaults)</code></pre>
    <p>
      Merging is <strong>most-conservative-wins</strong>, key by key:
      <code>dual</code> beats <code>single</code> beats <code>none</code>, and a
      component override may tighten the project policy but never relax it. An
      unknown value resolves to the most conservative mode, so an older plugin
      reading a newer policy degrades towards more gating, not less. Edits made
      while a pipeline runs apply from its next start.
    </p>
    <pre><code>schema_version: 2
gates:
  new-feature: dual      # dual | single | none   (absent => dual)
  bug: default           # default | none         (absent => default)
autonomy_log: true       # record every auto-resolved decision (zero-gate)
backlog_chaining: false  # chain the next open item after activation (zero-gate)</code></pre>

    <h2>The three modes</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Dual-gate</strong> (default, no policy) — two mandatory pauses
        in <code>/new-feature</code>: one on the requirement, one on the spec.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Single-gate</strong> — exactly one pause, with the requirement,
        the spec and a recommendation consolidated. Early escalation still stops
        before planning when something looks wrong.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Zero-gate</strong> (<code>none</code>) — no mandatory
        conversational pauses. The orchestrator runs <em>the same
        evaluation</em> as single-gate, but each signal that would ask for review
        is auto-resolved with the most conservative viable option and recorded
        in an <strong>AUTONOMY_LOG</strong>: one line per decision, shown in the
        progress output and saved with the pipeline's record in the knowledge
        base (or in the cancel reason if the transaction is cancelled first).
        Auditing replaces approval; it does not replace evaluation.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h2>The hard floor</h2>
    <p>
      Three classes always stop for a human, in every mode. They are not
      configurable — a floor you can lower is not a floor:
    </p>
    <ol>
      <li>
        any operation on the deploy no-go list — a production deploy, a real
        registry publish, a vault or allowlist change, a destructive git or
        cloud operation;
      </li>
      <li>an estimated recurring cost impact above 20% of the current budget;</li>
      <li>a security-tagged change that fails exhaustive validation.</li>
    </ol>
    <p>
      In zero-gate mode a validation criterion that fails three times does not
      ask: the pipeline records a blocked issue and continues when the criterion
      is not core, or cancels the transaction when it blocks the spec's core
      acceptance criteria.
    </p>

    <h2>Backlog chaining</h2>
    <p>
      With zero-gate and <code>backlog_chaining: true</code>, an invocation that
      declares a backlog scope — a milestone tag, a roadmap, or an explicit list
      of issues — picks the next open item after each activation and starts a
      fresh pipeline for it. It stops when the scope is empty, a hard-floor
      pause fires, two consecutive items end blocked, or the session budget runs
      out. Pacing belongs to the harness: run it under <code>/loop</code> (or
      scheduled wake-ups) for scopes that outlive one session; the skill itself
      never sleeps or busy-waits.
    </p>

    <h2>Rolling back</h2>
    <p>
      Set the policy's status to anything other than <code>active</code>, or
      archive it: the next pipeline start is back to the two default gates.
      There is no local file to clean up.
    </p>
